Please check our [Documentation](http://docs.lakekeeper.io), the [Lakekeeper Repository](https://github.com/lakekeeper/lakekeeper) and the [`values.yaml`](https://github.com/lakekeeper/lakekeeper-charts/blob/main/charts/lakekeeper/values.yaml) for more information.

## ⚠️ Important Notice: Migration from Bitnami to groundhog2k

This Chart used to rely on the Bitnami PostgreSQL Helm chart for postgres for both Lakekeeper and OpenFGA. Due to the [Bitnami Secure Images initiative](https://news.broadcom.com/app-dev/broadcom-introduces-bitnami-secure-images-for-production-ready-containerized-applications), this was no longer possible. We now moved to [`groundhog2k/posgres`](https://github.com/groundhog2k/helm-charts) with Chart Version `0.8.0`.

**Changes:**
- Version `0.7.1`: Transition from Bitnami images to self-hosted images on Quay.io to ensure deployment stability
- Version `0.8.0`: Migration to [`groundhog2k/posgres`](https://github.com/groundhog2k/helm-charts).

**Important notes:**
- No automatic migration will be provided by this chart
- **Back up your database before updating to prevent data loss**
- As noted in the installation section and [Production Checklist](https://docs.lakekeeper.io/docs/nightly/production/), the databases included with this chart are **not production-ready**

## Installing the Chart

To install the chart with the release name `my-release`:

```bash
helm repo add lakekeeper https://lakekeeper.github.io/lakekeeper-charts/
helm install my-lakekeeper lakekeeper/lakekeeper
```

⚠️ This chart by default deploys the "postgresql" subchart. This setup is not production ready. For production deployments, please deploy an external database, set `postgresql.enabled` to false and use the `externalDatabase.*` parameters to configure the connection. To deploy production ready Databases on Kubernetes, we recommend to use the [`CloudNativePGOperator`](https://cloudnative-pg.io/).

For potential additional steps that are required for upgrades, please check the [Changelog](./Changelog.md)

## ⚠️ Upgrading to Chart 0.13 (Lakekeeper 0.14)

The 0.14 migration rewrites table and view schemas and can take several minutes on large catalogs. Reads keep working: the 0.13 pods serve them until the migration is done, apart from a short pause at its end. Changes wait until then.

1. **Back up the database.** There is no downgrade to 0.13 without restoring it.
2. **Check for tables and views that would share a location.** This query must return no rows (see the [release notes](https://docs.lakekeeper.io/about/release-notes/) to fix them):
   ```sql
   SELECT warehouse_id, rtrim(fs_location, '/'), array_agg(tabular_id || ' ' || typ || ' ' || array_to_string(tabular_namespace_name, '.') || '.' || name || CASE WHEN deleted_at IS NOT NULL THEN ' (dropped)' ELSE '' END) FROM tabular GROUP BY 1, 2 HAVING count(*) > 1 AND count(DISTINCT fs_location) > 1;
   ```
3. **Check your configuration** against the breaking changes in the [Changelog](./Changelog.md).
4. **Upgrade** with a timeout that covers the migration:
   ```bash
   helm upgrade my-lakekeeper lakekeeper/lakekeeper --version 0.13.0 -f my-values.yaml --timeout 30m
   ```

**Optional: read-only mode.** To reject changes with `503` instead of letting them wait, switch the running 0.13 release to read-only first, then upgrade without the setting:
```bash
helm upgrade my-lakekeeper lakekeeper/lakekeeper --version 0.12.0 -f my-values.yaml \
  --set catalog.config.LAKEKEEPER__MAINTENANCE_MODE=read-only
kubectl rollout status deployment/my-lakekeeper
helm upgrade my-lakekeeper lakekeeper/lakekeeper --version 0.13.0 -f my-values.yaml --timeout 30m
```
In 0.13, read-only mode also rejects S3 remote signing and Trino (OPA bridge) requests. With Lakekeeper Plus, also set `LAKEKEEPER__TASK_EXPIRE_SNAPSHOTS_WORKERS` and `LAKEKEEPER__TASK_REMOVE_ORPHAN_FILES_WORKERS` to `0`.

To stop all 0.13 pods during the migration instead, upgrade with `--set catalog.strategy.type=Recreate`.

## Optimize Workers (Lakekeeper Plus)

With Lakekeeper Plus 0.15 or later, the chart can run [optimize](https://docs.lakekeeper.io/docs/latest/optimize/) workers as Kubernetes Jobs that [KEDA](https://keda.sh/) starts only while optimize tasks wait. Install KEDA in the cluster first, then set:

```yaml
lakekeeper:
  edition: plus
optimizeWorkers:
  enabled: true
```

The chart creates one KEDA ScaledJob per entry of `optimizeWorkers.sizeClasses`. The default is one class `small` with 2 CPUs, 8 GiB memory and at most 2 Jobs at the same time. The chart's `values.yaml` lists larger classes as comments. If your nodes have room for them, copy them into your `optimizeWorkers.sizeClasses` and keep `small`. By default, each Job requests 4 GiB of node disk per CPU for spilling (`optimizeWorkers.spill`). If your nodes have small disks, set `optimizeWorkers.spill.type: pvc`. With `optimizeWorkers.enabled: true`, the chart also sets `LAKEKEEPER__OPTIMIZE__AVAILABLE=true` on the catalog Pods and the Jobs. To choose the size classes and the other `optimizeWorkers.*` values, see [Optimize Workers](https://docs.lakekeeper.io/docs/latest/optimize-workers/).

Optimize itself is off by default. Turn it on for each warehouse that should use it, see [Turn It On](https://docs.lakekeeper.io/docs/latest/optimize/#turn-it-on).

KEDA reads the demand of each size class from port 9000 of the catalog Pods, through the Service `<fullname>-metrics`. If NetworkPolicies restrict traffic to the catalog Pods, allow the KEDA operator to reach this port.

Turning the workers off, renaming or removing a class, or uninstalling the chart deletes the ScaledJob of the class, and Kubernetes deletes its running Jobs with it.

Each worker Job opens up to 2 read and 2 write connections to Postgres (`optimizeWorkers.databasePoolConnections`). Make sure Postgres allows 4 more connections for every Job that can run at the same time (`keda.maxJobs` of each class).

With Helm 4, write CPU, memory and size values that reach the worker Jobs (`catalog.extraVolumes`, `optimizeWorkers.extraInitContainers`, `optimizeWorkers.extraVolumes`) as Kubernetes writes them, and quote numbers: `"1"`, `500m`, `1Gi`, not `1`, `1000m` or `1024Mi`. KEDA rewrites other forms, and the next `helm upgrade` fails with a conflict.

Lakekeeper can also run optimize in the catalog Pods (`LAKEKEEPER__TASK_OPTIMIZE_WORKERS`), but we do not recommend it: compaction needs a lot of memory and CPU, which the API then lacks.

